.env
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.idea/

# Per-machine assistant permission grants. Machine-specific and rewritten on
# every session; nothing here is part of the project.
.claude/

# Package and model caches kept inside the project so they land on drive F
# and disappear with a single `rm -rf .cache`.
.cache/

# Langfuse bind-mount data. Recreated by `docker compose up`.
docker/

# Local-only working areas, by explicit decision (see CLAUDE.md): the test
# suite and the working notes, plans, reports and diagram sources.
tests/
docs/

# Vector index and BM25 corpus. Rebuilt by `python ingest.py`; a committed
# index would go stale against data/ with no signal that it had.
index/

# Runtime logs and crash-recovery checkpoints. Post-mortem artefacts, not
# project history.
logs/
*.log
runtime/

# Markdown: only README.md and the final report are tracked. Agent-facing
# docs (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, insights.md, handoff.md) are
# local-only by explicit decision -- they never leave this machine, so every
# session must run from this same checkout.
*.md
!README.md
!report/*.md

# evals/ is a declared exception to the local-only doc policy above (spec
# Sec13, decision D5): the golden dataset and its fixtures ship publicly,
# alongside the rest of the tracked project. Without this negation,
# evals/fixtures/poisoned_document.md is silently swallowed by the *.md
# rule above -- found live, while staging this stage's own commit.
!evals/fixtures/*.md

# evals/analysis/ is local-only -- author's decision, 2026-08-20, reversing
# the visibility half of D9. The taxonomy, the 17 open-coding notes and
# fixes-pending.md are working prose of the same class as docs/, and the
# repository tracks artefacts a reader runs, not the reasoning behind them.
# D9's other half stands: the taxonomy still carries per-category counts and
# trace ids. What is lost is stated rather than glossed -- an outside reader
# can no longer check the coding, only the numbers derived from it.
evals/analysis/

# output/ holds generated reports, and they are a tracked, public deliverable:
# the reports are what this system produces, so a reader of the repository sees
# real ones rather than a placeholder. Ignore the directory's contents, then
# re-include the reports and the placeholder -- a stray file from a crashed run
# must not be picked up by a broad `git add`, and ignoring `output/` itself
# would make these negations unreachable.
#
# The negation is deliberately blanket, so the guard cannot live in whatever
# the committer remembers: `tests/test_output_reports_are_publishable.py`
# refuses any report carrying a system-prompt paragraph or the injection
# payload. Both are real, unfixed system defects (10b's F3 and F6), and a
# leaked report reaching GitHub is undoable by anything short of a history
# rewrite.
output/*
!output/.gitkeep
!output/*.md

# Stage-10b sweep artefacts (span dumps, packs, checkpoints, results) --
# per-run-set, rebuilt by `python -m evals.run_eval`, never committed. The one
# tracked output of a sweep is `evals/labels/human_labels.yaml`, written by
# hand from reading a run, not the run directory itself.
#
# This rule is load-bearing, not housekeeping: a sweep repoints OUTPUT_DIR at
# `runs/<set>/output/`, which is where the two reports carrying a leaked
# prompt and a successful injection actually landed. They stayed local by way
# of this line, not by way of anything the system itself decided.
runs/
